Uncomplicated aphthous ulcer (canker sore) on the buccal mucosa causing minor feeding discomfort
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Shallow buccal ulcer, Yellowish-gray pseudomembrane, Erythematous halo

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-048
Reason for Visit: Acute localized oral pain preventing normal feeding, requiring assessment to rule out severe infectious stomatitis or systemic viral illness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acutely painful mouth sore impairing oral intake. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to examine the oral mucosa, rule out disseminated herpes simplex or hand-foot-and-mouth disease, and ensure the patient was not at risk of acute dehydration due to pain-restricted fluid intake.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I have a sore on the inside of my cheek that burns like fire when I try to eat or drink.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a shallow, well-defined ulceration on the non-keratinized buccal mucosa that developed over the last 48 hours. Reports localized sharp pain, heavily exacerbated by acidic or salty foods. Notes the ulcer is small, less than one centimeter in diameter. Complete absence of underlying systemic fever, generalized malaise, or skin rashes. Denies recent major dental trauma, though reports a recent period of high stress and poor sleep.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but oral fluid intake is restricted due to pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Mild risk of dehydration if odynophagia continues to limit fluid intake.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/76 | HR: 74 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, speaking clearly but avoiding sharp cheek movements.
Physical Exam: Oral examination reveals a shallow well-defined ulceration on the buccal mucosa. The defect is covered by a central necrotic yellowish-gray pseudomembrane and is surrounded by a distinct erythematous halo. Diagnostic triad confirmed: shallow buccal ulcer, yellowish-gray pseudomembrane, and erythematous halo. Absence of extraoral cutaneous vesicles. Normal regional cervical lymph node examination without tender lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Acidic fruit juices visible in the kitchen.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking no regular immunosuppressants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Uncomplicated aphthous ulcer (canker sore) on the buccal mucosa causing minor feeding discomfort - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Herpes Simplex Stomatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Single isolated lesion rather than grouped vesicles on keratinized mucosa, and absence of systemic viral prodrome.

Oral Candidiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Single well-defined ulcer rather than diffuse white, scrapeable plaques.

Oral Squamous Cell Carcinoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Acute onset, extreme pain (early carcinomas are often painless), and absence of indurated, rolled borders.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with severe acute oral pain limiting fluid intake. An urgent after-hours physical examination was required to directly visualize the lesion and differentiate a benign, self-limiting ulcer from widespread infectious stomatitis, which might require antiviral therapy or intravenous hydration. The assessment safely confirmed an uncomplicated aphthous ulcer, averting an emergency department visit and allowing for targeted topical pain relief to restore adequate oral intake.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Recommendation for an over-the-counter topical anesthetic gel (e.g., benzocaine or choline salicylate) to be applied directly to the ulcer prior to meals. Use of a protective oral paste (e.g., Orabase) at night.
Positive Behavioral Change: Drink fluids through a straw placed at the back of the mouth, mechanically bypassing the painful ulcer on the buccal mucosa and ensuring adequate hydration without triggering the pain response.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if the ulcer fails to heal entirely within 3 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you become completely unable to swallow fluids, if you develop a high fever and a stiff neck, or if your throat swells.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; ulcers lasting longer than 3 weeks must be evaluated to rule out malignancy.
Clinical Course: The acute, sharp pain will peak over the next 2-3 days. Spontaneous resolution and healing are expected within 10 to 14 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop multiple similar sores down the back of your throat, or if the ulcer begins to bleed heavily and persistently.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A canker sore is a small, shallow defect in the lining of the mouth, often triggered by stress or minor trauma. The yellow center is dead tissue acting as a scab. Numbing gels provide temporary relief to allow you to eat.
Actionable Steps: Avoid acidic, spicy, or rough, sharp foods (like chips) that physically scrape the ulcer. Use the numbing gel 10 minutes before eating.

